      "target": "../sections/page-081.md",
      "narrative": null,
      "content": "69\nPlan the implementation\n5.1 \t Infrastructure considerations\nMany digital health applications and platforms have seen limited adoption and success because they \nwere piloted in areas that had inadequate access to electricity and mobile networks or were otherwise \ninappropriately designed for the context. Understanding the available infrastructure is foundational to defining \nthe scope and feasibility of the digital health application. Several practical approaches help alleviate constraints \nposed by infrastructural limitations. For example, erratic power supply and Internet connectivity may",
      "truncated": true,
      "provenance": "ingested",
      "summary": {
        "status": "draft",
        "text": "Section 5.1 ('Infrastructure considerations', page 69) guides assessing site readiness and contingency planning across four operational dimensions—Electricity (grid stability, seasonal outages, solar/battery alternatives, power management), Connectivity (voice/SMS/data coverage verification beyond MNO maps, offline storage capacity), Devices (local sourcing, end-user phone capabilities), and Digital Literacy and Language—and cites the BID Initiative Equipment support strategy in Box 5.1.1.",
