      "kind": "prose",
      "title": "Page 86",
      "pageStart": 86,
      "pageEnd": 86,
      "target": "../sections/page-086.md",
      "narrative": null,
      "content": "74\nDigital implementation investment guide\n5.4 \tWorkforce and training considerations\nChapter 2 listed the key governance, management and operations personnel needed for a digital health \nimplementation. At this point, you will focus on the workforce directly involved in the digital health enterprise, \nsuch as community and facility health workers and healthcare managers. Although some health workers will \nshow great enthusiasm for new technologies, introducing and institutionalizing the intervention with all of the \nworkforce or client end-users can be challenging. \nEstablish training program",
      "truncated": true,
      "provenance": "ingested",
      "summary": {
        "status": "draft",
        "text": "Section 5.4 ('Workforce and training considerations', page 74) details strategies for training and supporting health workers and managers—including introductory and refresher training, training-of-trainers models, usage metadata monitoring, champions, managing dual paper-and-digital data entry during phased transitions, culturally adaptable UI design, feeding data back to frontline workers to foster a data-use culture, and password/privacy hygiene.",
        "state": "draft",
        "draftedBy": {
          "kind": "agent",
          "id": "claude-code",
          "model": "not-disclosed"
