      "pageEnd": 87,
      "target": "../sections/page-087.md",
      "narrative": null,
      "content": "75\nPlan the implementation\nBID INITIATIVE\nCASE STUDY\nBID Initiative: Workforce implementation\nThe BID Initiative developed a workforce implementation strategy (80) informed by John Kotter’s eight-step \nmodel for change management (see Fig. 5.4.1). “Touches”, or visits, were used to introduce and provide training \non new tools and approaches, allowing health workers time to internalize the practice of data analysis and \nuse (see Fig. 5.4.2). Successful implementation requires buy-in from local health workers, so BID centred its \napproach on health workers, both as trainers and trainees. Instead",
      "truncated": true,
      "provenance": "ingested",
      "summary": {
        "status": "draft",
        "text": "BID Initiative case study ('BID Initiative: Workforce implementation', page 75) explaining how BID used district immunization officers to lead facility-level training 'touches' and presenting Figure 5.4.1, which maps John Kotter's eight-step change management model across Creating the climate for change (Steps 1–3), Engaging and enabling the organization (Steps 4–6), and Implementing and sustaining for change (Steps 7–8).",
