      "pageEnd": 90,
      "target": "../sections/page-090.md",
      "narrative": null,
      "content": "78\nDigital implementation investment guide\n\t» Throughout this process, put in place mechanisms \nto collect and respond to end-user issues, \nparticularly in the early stages of piloting and \ndeployment. This mechanism will need to describe \nhow end-users are expected to report problems, \nwho investigates and responds, and how quickly \nthey should respond. \nEnsure that the health content is in line with evidence-\nbased recommendations and best practices. Based on \nthe health programme area where your digital health \nintervention is focused, consult relevant resources and \ncountry experts to ensu",
      "truncated": true,
      "provenance": "ingested",
      "summary": {
        "status": "draft",
        "text": "Concludes Section 5.5 (page 78) on establishing issue-reporting mechanisms and aligning embedded clinical algorithms and indicators with evidence-based guidelines (including WHO Digital Accelerator Kits and Computable Guidelines Standards), and presents Figure 5.5.1 comparing the benefits, risks, and real-world examples of five software models: Custom-Developed Software, Commercial Off-the-Shelf (COTS) Software, Free Packaged Software, Open Source Software, and Software as a Service (SaaS).",
