      "target": "../sections/page-091.md",
      "narrative": null,
      "content": "79\nPlan the implementation\n5.6 \tStandards and interoperability \nStandards and interoperability are also critical factors for ensuring the success of digital health \nimplementations. For different systems to be interoperable, or to meaningfully exchange information with one \nanother, procedures and data standards must be established that ensure a common language and facilitate data \nexchange. Interoperability is not only critical for driving cost efficiencies and reducing fragmentation across \ndifferent digital systems, but is also necessary to support the continuity of care as patients engage",
      "truncated": true,
      "provenance": "ingested",
      "summary": {
        "status": "draft",
        "text": "Presents Section 5.6 (Standards and interoperability), explaining why procedures and data standards are essential to maintain continuity of care and avoid fragmented systems, with Box 5.6.1 outlining general considerations (linking to national identification registries such as health workforce, client, and master facility registries, and adopting health data exchange standards such as HL7, ICD, and IHE), followed by Section 5.7 (National digital health strategy and investment plan) on reviewing alignment with national digital health strategies and investment plans.",
