    {
      "id": "library/9789240010567-eng/blocks/prose-page-114",
      "types": [
        "folio-assistant-core:Prose",
        "doco:Section"
      ],
      "kind": "prose",
      "title": "Page 114",
      "pageStart": 114,
      "pageEnd": 114,
      "target": "../sections/page-114.md",
      "narrative": null,
      "content": "102\nDigital implementation investment guide\nPRINCIPLES FOR \nDIGITAL DEVELOPMENT\n \nBE DATA \nDRIVEN1\n\t+ Design programmes so that impact can be measured continually and \nincrementally, focusing on outcomes, not just outputs.\n\t+ Make use of existing data, including open data sets and data from interoperable \nsystems.\n\t+ Use rigorous data collection methods. Consider and address potential biases \nand gaps in the data collected, perform data quality checks, and maintain strong \ndocumentation behind collected data.\n\t+ Close knowledge gaps by contributing data to the development community and \nusing",
      "truncated": true,
      "provenance": "ingested",
      "summary": {
        "status": "draft",
        "text": "Summarizes the Principles for Digital Development tenet Be Data Driven, outlining ten core practices including continual outcome-focused measurement, leveraging open and interoperable data, rigorous bias-aware data collection and quality checks, closing knowledge gaps, using timely data for rapid decision-making, presenting actionable visualizations, building stakeholder data-use capacity, holistic collaborative analysis, open standards, and responsible data governance.",
        "state": "draft",
        "draftedBy": {
          "kind": "agent",
          "id": "claude-code",
          "model": "not-disclosed"
        },
        "draftedAt": "2026-10-30"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "library/9789240010567-eng/blocks/prose-page-115",
      "types": [
        "folio-assistant-core:Prose",
        "doco:Section"
      ],
      "kind": "prose",
      "title": "Page 115",
      "pageStart": 115,
      "pageEnd": 115,
      "target": "../sections/page-115.md",
      "narrative": null,
      "content": "103\nMonitor the implementation and use data effectively\nAfter going through the steps to plan the digital health implementation but prior to deploying the \nimplementation, you can embed mechanisms to monitor the implementation, such as collecting baseline data, \nand use the insights from the emerging data to increase your implementation’s impact and efficiency. Continual \nmonitoring of activities at different stages of the implementation is critical for ensuring its long-term success, as \nis responding to external changes and new learnings. \nFig. 8.1.\t\nM&E and adaptive management as continual",
      "truncated": true,
      "provenance": "ingested",
      "summary": {
        "status": "draft",
        "text": "Opens Chapter 8’s narrative on embedding baseline data collection and continual monitoring across implementation stages, accompanied by Figure 8.1 (M&E and adaptive management as continual considerations for digital health implementations across the seven WHO/ITU eHealth building blocks), and introduces the chapter’s three interrelated concepts: monitoring and evaluation, establishing a culture of data use, and adaptive management.",
        "state": "draft",
        "draftedBy": {
          "kind": "agent",
          "id": "claude-code",
          "model": "not-disclosed"
        },
        "draftedAt": "2026-10-30"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "library/9789240010567-eng/blocks/prose-page-116",
      "types": [
        "folio-assistant-core:Prose",
        "doco:Section"
      ],
      "kind": "prose",
      "title": "Page 116",
      "pageStart": 116,
      "pageEnd": 116,
      "target": "../sections/page-116.md",
      "narrative": null,
      "content": "104\nDigital implementation investment guide\n8.1 \t Establish a logic model for your implementation\nDefining a framework in the form of a logic model will \nhelp you refine and understand your programme’s goals \nand objectives and conceptualize the relationship \nbetween them, the underpinning programme activities \nrequired to achieve them and the anticipated outcomes. \nLogic models aim to clarify programme objectives and \naid in identifying expected causal links from inputs, \nprocesses, outputs, outcomes and impacts (4). They \nprovide a graphical representation, which may serve as \na catalyst for",
      "truncated": true,
      "provenance": "ingested",
      "summary": {
        "status": "draft",
        "text": "Begins Section 8.1 (Establish a logic model for your implementation), explaining how logic models clarify programme objectives and map causal links from inputs and processes to outputs, outcomes, and impacts, and presents Figure 8.1.1 (Illustrative logic model of MomConnect digital health investment in South Africa), detailing partnerships, health promotion messaging, facility/community inputs, technology, funding, system utilization, health service outcomes, and mortality reductions.",
        "state": "draft",
        "draftedBy": {
          "kind": "agent",
          "id": "claude-code",
          "model": "not-disclosed"
        },
        "draftedAt": "2026-10-30"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "library/9789240010567-eng/blocks/prose-page-117",
      "types": [
        "folio-assistant-core:Prose",
        "doco:Section"
      ],
      "kind": "prose",
      "title": "Page 117",
      "pageStart": 117,
      "pageEnd": 117,
      "target": "../sections/page-117.md",
      "narrative": null,
      "content": "105\nMonitor the implementation and use data effectively\nLogic models link inputs (programme resources) with \nprocesses (activities undertaken in the delivery of \nservices), outputs (products of processes), outcomes \n(intermediate changes) and impact. \nIn the context of digital health implementations, \nINPUTS encompass all resources that go into the \nprogramme. In this model, technology inputs are \ndifferentiated from programmatic inputs aimed at \nproviding health services. Programmatic inputs (human \nresources, training and other materials development) \nare also distinguished from policy input",
      "truncated": true,
      "provenance": "ingested",
      "summary": {
        "status": "draft",
        "text": "Concludes Section 8.1 by defining each component of a digital health logic model: Inputs (technology, programmatic human-resource/training, and policy/affordability resources), Processes (technology capacity-building and platform enhancements, national implementation strategy formulation, health worker training/supervision, and information security), Outputs (technological adoption/performance and service delivery improvements), Outcomes (across health system, health worker, and client levels), and Impact (health system performance, morbidity/mortality reductions, and reduced out-of-pocket catastrophic costs).",
        "state": "draft",
        "draftedBy": {
          "kind": "agent",
          "id": "claude-code",
          "model": "not-disclosed"
        },
        "draftedAt": "2026-10-30"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "library/9789240010567-eng/blocks/prose-page-118",
      "types": [
        "folio-assistant-core:Prose",
        "doco:Section"
      ],
      "kind": "prose",
      "title": "Page 118",
      "pageStart": 118,
      "pageEnd": 118,
      "target": "../sections/page-118.md",
      "narrative": null,
      "content": "106\nDigital implementation investment guide\n8.2 \tPlan how you will conduct the \nmonitoring and evaluation\nPlanning to deploy a digital health intervention includes \ndetermining what to monitor to ensure that the \nintervention is working as planned and what to evaluate \nto ensure that it is having the effects you expected. \nThese efforts may include tracking performance, \nchanges in processes, health outcomes, end-user \nsatisfaction with health systems, cost-effectiveness or \nshifts in knowledge and attitudes. \nMONITORING helps answer the question: Is the \nintervention working as intended? Moni",
      "truncated": true,
      "provenance": "ingested",
      "summary": {
        "status": "draft",
        "text": "Begins Section 8.2 (Plan how you will conduct the monitoring and evaluation), distinguishing Monitoring (routinely tracking whether the intervention works as intended, evolving from code/usability checks to field fidelity, quality, system integration, and policy compliance at scale) from Evaluation (systematically assessing whether objectives and health outcomes are achieved, evolving from early effect assessments on clinical adherence and timeliness to health economic evaluations, return on investment, and implementation research).",
        "state": "draft",
        "draftedBy": {
          "kind": "agent",
          "id": "claude-code",
          "model": "not-disclosed"
        },
        "draftedAt": "2026-10-30"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "library/9789240010567-eng/blocks/prose-page-119",
      "types": [
        "folio-assistant-core:Prose",
        "doco:Section"
      ],
      "kind": "prose",
      "title": "Page 119",
      "pageStart": 119,
      "pageEnd": 119,
      "target": "../sections/page-119.md",
      "narrative": null,
      "content": "107\nMonitor the implementation and use data effectively\nIdeally, M&E occur in close balance with each other \nand are structured to answer questions that are most \nrelevant at each stage of the implementation. At the \nearly stages, you could use M&E results to iteratively \nredesign and test the digital health intervention \nto better meet the needs of end-users and the \norganization. Fig. 8.2.1 illustrates the evolution of M&E \nneeds during the development and deployment stages \nof implementation.\nFig. 8.2.1. \t Intervention maturity over time.\nMaturity models can serve as measuring sticks or \nin",
      "truncated": true,
      "provenance": "ingested",
      "summary": {
        "status": "draft",
        "text": "Continues Section 8.2 by explaining how M&E activities evolve alongside intervention maturity from prototype to national implementation, illustrated by Figure 8.2.1 (Intervention maturity over time, mapping monitoring of functionality, stability, fidelity, and quality alongside evaluation of usability, feasibility, efficacy, effectiveness, implementation research, and economic/financial evaluation).",
        "state": "draft",
        "draftedBy": {
          "kind": "agent",
          "id": "claude-code",
          "model": "not-disclosed"
        },
        "draftedAt": "2026-10-30"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "library/9789240010567-eng/blocks/prose-page-120",
      "types": [
        "folio-assistant-core:Prose",
        "doco:Section"
      ],
      "kind": "prose",
      "title": "Page 120",
      "pageStart": 120,
      "pageEnd": 120,
      "target": "../sections/page-120.md",
      "narrative": null,
      "content": "108\nDigital implementation investment guide\nFig. 8.2.2. \t Implementation maturity continuum.\nSource: Adapted from WHO Monitoring and evaluating digital health interventions, 2016 (26).\n \nComponent\nWhen\nPotential Measures\nImplementation Maturity Continuum \n& Guiding Questions\nQUALITY\nPre-launch \n& during \nimplementation\n•\t End-user entry of phone number \nis correct\n•\t Rate of agreement in data \nrecording between training rounds \n(i.e. end-user accuracy)\n•\t Quality control reports on \nend-users\nHow well and consistently are \nend-end-users able to operate \nthe system?\nAre the content and use of t",
      "truncated": true,
      "provenance": "ingested",
      "summary": {
        "status": "draft",
        "text": "Presents Figure 8.2.2 (Implementation maturity continuum, adapted from the 2016 WHO M&E guide), detailing timing (pre-launch versus during implementation), potential measures, and guiding questions across four core monitoring components: Functionality (SMS/form content, schedules, QA test adherence), Stability (server downtime, SMS failure rate, network connectivity), Fidelity (stability/functionality reports, device loss, connectivity/power outages, password issues), and Quality (data recording accuracy, quality control reports, form timestamps, submission patterns).",
        "state": "draft",
        "draftedBy": {
          "kind": "agent",
          "id": "claude-code",
          "model": "not-disclosed"
        },
        "draftedAt": "2026-10-30"
      }
