    {
      "id": "library/9789240010567-eng/blocks/prose-page-122",
      "types": [
        "folio-assistant-core:Prose",
        "doco:Section"
      ],
      "kind": "prose",
      "title": "Page 122",
      "pageStart": 122,
      "pageEnd": 122,
      "target": "../sections/page-122.md",
      "narrative": null,
      "content": "110\nDigital implementation investment guide\nThe RE-AIM framework may also be considered as a \ncomprehensive way to think through your M&E needs as \nyou scale up the implementation. The following are the \ncomponents of the RE-AIM framework (98):\nReach: the level of penetration of an intervention in \nterms of the proportion of eligible participants who \nreceive the intervention \nEffectiveness: the impact on targeted outcomes, \nincluding potential negative effects\nAdoption: the absolute number and proportion of \norganizational units, individuals or settings that adopt a \ngiven intervention\nImplem",
      "truncated": true,
      "provenance": "ingested",
      "summary": {
        "status": "draft",
        "text": "Concludes Section 8.2 by introducing the five dimensions of the RE-AIM framework (Reach, Effectiveness, Adoption, Implementation, and Maintenance) for scaling M&E, and begins Section 8.3 (Establish a culture of data use), outlining how credible M&E data, empowered staff, supportive managers, and leadership data policies foster data-driven decision-making (referencing examples in Fig. 8.3.1).",
        "state": "draft",
        "draftedBy": {
          "kind": "agent",
          "id": "claude-code",
          "model": "not-disclosed"
        },
        "draftedAt": "2026-10-30"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "library/9789240010567-eng/blocks/prose-page-123",
      "types": [
        "folio-assistant-core:Prose",
        "doco:Section"
      ],
      "kind": "prose",
      "title": "Page 123",
      "pageStart": 123,
      "pageEnd": 123,
      "target": "../sections/page-123.md",
      "narrative": null,
      "content": "111\nMonitor the implementation and use data effectively\nFig. 8.3.1. \t Examples of data use.\nBENEFIT\nEXAMPLES\nHOW TO EVALUATE SUCCESS\nBETTER INDICATORS \nFOR STRATEGIC \nPLANNING\n•\t Targeted advocacy efforts help to address higher-than-\naverage vaccination dropout rates in specific population \ngroups.\n•\t Credible estimates of vaccine wastage rates for each \nhealth centre lead to tailored vaccination strategies to \nreduce wastage.\n•\t High failure rates in certain types of cold chain equipment \nlead to the discontinuation of that equipment.\n•\t Did the system produce credible data for these \nindicat",
      "truncated": true,
      "provenance": "ingested",
      "summary": {
        "status": "draft",
        "text": "Presents Figure 8.3.1 (Examples of data use), mapping concrete health system examples and evaluation questions across four benefit areas (Better indicators for strategic planning, Better day-to-day decision-making, Better control and oversight, and Reduced administrative burden), followed by Box 8.3.2 summarizing the Immunization Data: Evidence for Action (IDEA) realist review of nearly 550 documents on strengthening immunization data use.",
        "state": "draft",
        "draftedBy": {
          "kind": "agent",
          "id": "claude-code",
          "model": "not-disclosed"
        },
        "draftedAt": "2026-10-30"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "library/9789240010567-eng/blocks/prose-page-124",
      "types": [
        "folio-assistant-core:Prose",
        "doco:Section"
      ],
      "kind": "prose",
      "title": "Page 124",
      "pageStart": 124,
      "pageEnd": 124,
      "target": "../sections/page-124.md",
      "narrative": null,
      "content": "112\nDigital implementation investment guide\nAlthough the high quality of data is important, quality \ndoes not guarantee data use at an individual, facility, \ncommunity or organizational level. Additionally, while \na trained cadre of data end-users is critical, data must \nmeet the requirements of multiple end-users and \nend-user scenarios, informing both national policy and \nservice delivery among health workers. Furthermore, \nthe right people (at all levels) must be able to get the \ninformation they need, when they need it, for their \npurposes. \nData must be translated into information at the",
      "truncated": true,
      "provenance": "ingested",
      "summary": {
        "status": "draft",
        "text": "Continues Section 8.3 by explaining that data must be aggregated and translated into actionable information tailored to decision-makers at each health system level (from facility nurses tracking vaccine defaulters in an EIR to district and national managers monitoring coverage targets), and presents Figure 8.3.3 (Data-driven accountability cycle: Quality Data → Understandable Information → Actionable Message → Action (Policy or Program) → Data Demand).",
        "state": "draft",
        "draftedBy": {
          "kind": "agent",
          "id": "claude-code",
          "model": "not-disclosed"
        },
        "draftedAt": "2026-10-30"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "library/9789240010567-eng/blocks/prose-page-125",
      "types": [
        "folio-assistant-core:Prose",
        "doco:Section"
      ],
      "kind": "prose",
      "title": "Page 125",
      "pageStart": 125,
      "pageEnd": 125,
      "target": "../sections/page-125.md",
      "narrative": null,
      "content": "113\nMonitor the implementation and use data effectively\nFour other factors can help complete cultural change, as described in Table 8.3.4.\nTable 8.3.4. \t\nCultural change factors.\nAwareness of \nneed\nEarlier, you identified your health system challenges, along with the information needed to \naddress these challenges. Implementing an awareness campaign that builds a case for action \nappropriate across different levels of the health system can increase support for the digital \nhealth intervention.\nMotivation to \nact\nMotivation can come from both external drivers (such as job performance indicators",
      "truncated": true,
      "provenance": "ingested",
      "summary": {
        "status": "draft",
        "text": "Presents Table 8.3.4 (Cultural change factors: Awareness of need, Motivation to act, Empowerment to act, and Skills to use and improve quality) and begins a BID Initiative case study (BID Initiative: Building a culture of data use), describing how automated EIR reporting, monthly facility dashboards, WhatsApp peer-support forums, data-use guides, and supportive supervision strengthened data use in Tanzania, illustrated by Figure 8.3.5 (The data journey, with and without data-use interventions).",
        "state": "draft",
        "draftedBy": {
          "kind": "agent",
          "id": "claude-code",
          "model": "not-disclosed"
        },
        "draftedAt": "2026-10-30"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "library/9789240010567-eng/blocks/prose-page-126",
      "types": [
        "folio-assistant-core:Prose",
        "doco:Section"
      ],
      "kind": "prose",
      "title": "Page 126",
      "pageStart": 126,
      "pageEnd": 126,
      "target": "../sections/page-126.md",
      "narrative": null,
      "content": "114\nDigital implementation investment guide\nThe following are key learnings from implementing these data-use interventions in Tanzania and Zambia. \n\t» Teach data-analysis skills with the facility’s existing data to help nurses identify challenges \ncurrently affecting service delivery and pinpoint ways to address those issues. This foundation in \ndata analysis better prepares nurses to adopt new tools and to adapt their data-analysis skill sets to \ndifferent service areas, such as malaria.\n\t» Electronic tools, as well as revised paper forms, must go through an iterative process with \nfeedback f",
      "truncated": true,
      "provenance": "ingested",
      "summary": {
        "status": "draft",
        "text": "Concludes the BID Initiative data-use case study by summarizing five key learnings from Tanzania and Zambia—teaching data-analysis skills using existing facility data, iterating electronic and paper tools with User Advisory Groups (UAGs), using supportive supervision and dashboards to assist low-performing facilities, creating district WhatsApp peer-support networks, and engaging regional, provincial, and national stakeholders—and referencing BID’s Spot check form and Data-use culture job aid.",
        "state": "draft",
        "draftedBy": {
          "kind": "agent",
          "id": "claude-code",
          "model": "not-disclosed"
        },
        "draftedAt": "2026-10-30"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "library/9789240010567-eng/blocks/prose-page-127",
      "types": [
        "folio-assistant-core:Prose",
        "doco:Section"
      ],
      "kind": "prose",
      "title": "Page 127",
      "pageStart": 127,
      "pageEnd": 127,
      "target": "../sections/page-127.md",
      "narrative": null,
      "content": "115\nMonitor the implementation and use data effectively\n8.4 \tAdaptive management: \nUse data to optimize interventions \nAdaptive management requires realizing that change \nhappens and building in the ability to respond to the \nchange (106). Emerging from the interdisciplinary \nneed and understanding that complex development \nissues require nimble solutions, adaptive management \ncalls for incremental, steady iteration (107). Adaptive \nmanagement ensures that M&E plan outputs are \ncontinuously used to improve the digital health \nintervention or larger digital health investment.\nAdaptive managemen",
      "truncated": true,
      "provenance": "ingested",
      "summary": {
        "status": "draft",
        "text": "Begins Section 8.4 (Adaptive management: Use data to optimize interventions), explaining how real-time M&E data and continuous learning cycles support iterative course correction and mitigate organizational resistance to data transformation, accompanied by Table 8.4.1 (Traditional versus adaptive management) and the first five guiding questions for developing an adaptive management plan.",
        "state": "draft",
        "draftedBy": {
          "kind": "agent",
          "id": "claude-code",
          "model": "not-disclosed"
        },
        "draftedAt": "2026-10-30"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "library/9789240010567-eng/blocks/prose-page-128",
      "types": [
        "folio-assistant-core:Prose",
        "doco:Section"
      ],
      "kind": "prose",
      "title": "Page 128",
      "pageStart": 128,
      "pageEnd": 128,
      "target": "../sections/page-128.md",
      "narrative": null,
      "content": "116\nDigital implementation investment guide\n\t» Does your team have mechanisms for translating \nlearnings into change? If so, how do you integrate \nchange at an implementation, programme or \norganizational level?\n\t» What tools and mechanisms does your team \nuse to determine the appropriate approach to \ninterventions (such as theories of change, benefit \nanalyses, stakeholder analyses and so on)?\n\t» How do you evaluate progress, taking into account \nuncertainties and repeated cycles of learning and \nchange?\n\t» How do you analyse and communicate results?\nA full checklist of considerations can be",
      "truncated": true,
      "provenance": "ingested",
      "summary": {
        "status": "draft",
        "text": "Concludes Section 8.4 with the remaining four guiding questions for adaptive management (referencing the full checklist in Annex 8.1), explains how to schedule regular and milestone-driven \"pause-and-reflect\" reviews with stakeholders to document decisions and redirect when needed, and presents Figure 8.4.2 (Adaptive management cycle: Act → Review → Pause and reflect → Adapt).",
        "state": "draft",
        "draftedBy": {
          "kind": "agent",
          "id": "claude-code",
          "model": "not-disclosed"
        },
        "draftedAt": "2026-10-30"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "library/9789240010567-eng/blocks/prose-page-129",
      "types": [
        "folio-assistant-core:Prose",
        "doco:Section"
      ],
      "kind": "prose",
      "title": "Page 129",
      "pageStart": 129,
      "pageEnd": 129,
      "target": "../sections/page-129.md",
      "narrative": null,
      "content": "117\nMonitor the implementation and use data effectively\n8.5 \tProgress check \nAt this point in the \nprocess, you should \nhave the following \noutputs:\n\t\n Problem statement detailing specific challenges and needs in \nthe health system\n\t\n Identified digital health interventions to address the current \nchallenges\n\t\n Enabling-environment assessment defining possible constraints\n\t\n Implementation plan that is appropriate for the environmental \nlimitations\n\t\n Linkages of this specific investment to the broader set of digital \nhealth activities and enterprise architecture\n\t\n High-level financial",
      "truncated": true,
      "provenance": "ingested",
      "summary": {
        "status": "draft",
        "text": "Presents Section 8.5 (Progress check), a seven-item checklist confirming that the implementation team has produced a problem statement of health system challenges, identified digital health interventions, an enabling-environment assessment, an appropriate implementation plan, linkages to the broader enterprise architecture, a high-level financial plan and costing, and an M&E plan covering adaptive management and data use.",
        "state": "draft",
        "draftedBy": {
          "kind": "agent",
          "id": "claude-code",
          "model": "not-disclosed"
        },
        "draftedAt": "2026-10-30"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "library/9789240010567-eng/blocks/prose-page-130",
      "types": [
        "folio-assistant-core:Prose",
        "doco:Section"
      ],
